**On translation from WCE to OSM**

I. WCE -> OSM is often many-to-one (the easy translation direction)
    A. Vowel mergers in OSM
        1. OSM treats ə and ʌ as a single sound, close to schwa, as the difference isn't significant in Western Canadian English (WCE).
        2. Because WCE features the cot-caught merger ɒ, ɑː, and ɔː are treated as a single sound in OSM.
        3. WCE is yod dropping. So, ju(ː) and variants have no place in OSM.
    B. Consonant mergers in OSM
        1. The wine-whine merger
        2. Yod dropping also applies to many consonant phonemes (but OSM has none)


II. OSM -> WCE is often one-to-many (the hard translation direction)

