Typographically speaking:

    A. Unvoiced and unvoiced phonemes are indicated by a glyph's relationship to the baseline:
        1. Glyphs for unvoiced consonants are entirely above the baseline and have at least one ascender.
        2. Glyphs for voiced consonants extend below the baseline (at least one descender) and have no ascenders.
        3. Glyphs for vowels, excepting r-colored vowel glyphs, have neither ascenders nor descenders.

I am interested in expanding the aesthetic choice of glyphs beyond Shavian letters (however, I suspect that some of them are perfect for my project... even if some are currently serving the wrong roles). The reason is because, in addition to using ascenders and descenders as a way to identify consonants and associating voiced phonemes with the area around the baseline (i.e. "down"), I would like to add additional guidelines and suggest that you are free to consider any glyph that Google Noto can render. For this reason, you should also re-consider my original rules to see if there are better options in Noto. They were:

1. Glyphs for unvoiced consonants are entirely above the baseline and have at least one ascender.
2. Glyphs for voiced consonants extend below the baseline (at least one descender) and have no ascenders.
3. Glyphs for vowels, excepting r-colored vowel glyphs, have neither ascenders nor descenders.

Here are my additional guidelines:

About consonants:
a. Bilabial, labio-dental, and dental glyphs should "point toward the left" and be left-to-right asymmetrical with more glyph on the left (I suppose the numbers "7" and "9" are examples of such glyphs).
b. Alveolar consonants should have left-to-right symmetry.
c. Palatal, velar, and glottal glyphs should "point toward the right" and be left-to-right asymmetrical with more glyph on the right (I suppose "P" and "b" are examples of such glyphs).
d. Consonants that are stops, fricatives or affricatives should have a more kiki than bouba appearance (with stops being the most kiki).
e. Consonants that are glides, nasals, or liquids should have a more bouba than kiki appearance (with nasals being the most bouba). 

About vowels:
Within the x-height, the highlight or focus of the glyph should approximate the vowel's position on the IPA close-to-open/front-to-back vowel chart . It seems appropriate for all vowel glyphs to be "bottom heavy" because they are all voiced.

Ensure there are no internally inconsistent implications in my set of guidelines.


